<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> was the seizure of these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a>-producing islands in 1621 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Jan Pieterszoon Coen</a>. Most of the native population was killed, enslaved or driven out, and the islands were resettled with plantations worked by enslaved labour, securing a Dutch monopoly on nutmeg and mace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-conquest-of-the-banda-islands.jpg" alt="Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaart_van_de_Banda-eilanden,_1599,_RP-P-OB-75.391.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The volcanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> were the only source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and mace. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet reached them in 1599, and Jacob van Heemskerck signed contracts with Bandanese chiefs. The Bandanese preferred free trade, selling to the highest bidder, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), founded on 20 March 1602, wanted a monopoly. In late May 1609 chiefs lured the commander Pieter Willemsz Verhoeff into an ambush, killing 46 Dutchmen in all, Verhoeff among them; the Dutch plundered villages and imposed a treaty, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nassau-gold-coast">Fort Nassau</a> on Banda Neira.</p><p>The Bandanese went on selling to English, Malay, Javanese and Makassarese traders, and by 1614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heeren-xvii">Heeren XVII</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>'s governing body, resolved to conquer the whole archipelago. In April 1616 Jan Dirkszoon Lam took Ai, where about 400 islanders, many of them women and children, drowned fleeing to Run, then held by the English. The English under Nathaniel Courthope held Run for over four years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 26 October 1620 Governor-General Jan Pieterszoon Coen wrote that Banda must be subjugated again and populated with other people. His fleet of 19 ships, with 1,655 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> soldiers and 286 Japanese mercenaries, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nassau-gold-coast">Fort Nassau</a> on 21 February 1621. Attacking Lontor, the main island, on 11 March, it held the whole island by the end of 12 March. The orang kaya, Lontor's upper class, accepted Dutch sovereignty, but many islanders fled to the hills and fought on. After confessions extracted by torture on 21 April, Coen deported at least 789 orang kaya and their families to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, and 24 were beheaded by the Japanese mercenaries on 8 May. Villages were destroyed, and many islanders starved or leapt from the cliffs rather than surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Coen</a> himself reported that about 2,500 died of hunger and misery or by the sword. The historian Hans Straver estimated that of 4,500 to 5,000 people on Lontor, 1,700 were enslaved and 2,500 died of famine and disease, while several hundred escaped to the Kei Islands and eastern Seram. Vincent Loth and Charles Corn put the islands' population before the conquest at about 15,000, of whom only 1,000 survived, and Peter Lape estimated that 90% were killed, enslaved or deported. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> planters (perkeniers) worked the islands with slaves from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; the native Bandanese numbered 100 by 1681. The English renounced their claim in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> of 1667.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the Maluku Islands, covering nutmeg, cloves and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the <b>Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands</b> under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
